<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Explored the Ecological History Of...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Summarize arguments from general and academic articles. Reading: Analyze authentic non-fiction texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can summarize and evaluate arguments in articles about local governance and civic engagement, focusing on diverse viewpoints (e.g., "This article highlights that participation in town meetings can lead to better street repairs, but others argue that online surveys could be more inclusive.")
            Can analyze authentic texts about environmental restoration activities, evaluating their impact on the environment (e.g., "Planting trees along the river has improved air quality and reduced erosion.")
            Can extract main points from articles on forest health and share them in reviews, noting environmental improvements (e.g., "Our community's recycling program has successfully reduced waste in local landfills.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various forest conditions: lush, healthy forests; areas affected by deforestation; and areas undergoing environmental restoration. Students briefly discuss in pairs what they observe, focusing on changes over time and their potential causes or effects. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What changes do you see in these forests?" "What might have caused these changes?" This activates prior knowledge related to 'Our Changing Forests' and the target vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, ecosystems).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models strategies for reading non-fiction texts for main arguments. For example, the teacher takes a short sample paragraph (not the main article for the lesson) and demonstrates how to identify the topic sentence, scan for keywords (e.g., *deforestation, impact, reduce, improve*), and highlight supporting details. The teacher also models reading aloud sentences containing the long vowel sound /aɪ/ (e.g., "marine <b>life</b>", "<b>bio</b>diversity") and words with reduced /ɪt/ endings (e.g., "<b>climate</b>", "approximate").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we read for the main idea, are we looking for every single detail or the most important message?" (Expected answer: The most important message.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'biodiversity' mean in the context of forests?" (Expected answer: The variety of life, like different species of plants and animals.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic non-fiction excerpt (approx. 250-300 words) from an environmental news report or a science blog post titled "Panama's Changing Forests: A History of Impact and Conservation." The text includes instances of Present Perfect ("Deforestation *has significantly affected* biodiversity in our area."), First and Second Conditional ("If we *preserve* our forests, we *will help* reduce deforestation."), Comparatives ("Urban ecosystems *have less biodiversity compared to* rural habitats."), and the target vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, rivers, ecosystems, conservation, forest health, logging, species, impact, reduce, plant, improve, protect).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting and understanding written texts about environmental issues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the article silently for the first time.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main topic of this article?" and "What overall message does the author want to convey about Panama's forests?" Students write their answers individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students answer 3-4 True/False statements about general information in the text (e.g., "The article suggests that logging has always been sustainable in Panama.") and correct the false statements based on the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for specific information, identifying arguments, and understanding the use of present perfect, conditionals, and comparatives within the context of environmental issues. Reinforcement of target vocabulary (e.g., *deforestation, conservation, impact, protect, recover, reduce, improve, biodiversity, air quality, climate change*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Argument Identification:</b> Students re-read the article more carefully. In pairs, they identify and underline sentences that present a main argument or a key impact described by the author. They also circle examples of Present Perfect structures ("Deforestation *has significantly affected* biodiversity") and conditional sentences ("If we *protect* these areas, local *species will recover* faster."). The teacher monitors and assists with grammar and vocabulary, particularly with the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "dramatic" and "ecological."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Analyzing Textual Evidence:</b> Students complete a graphic organizer individually, dividing the article into sections and noting the main argument of each section, followed by at least two pieces of supporting evidence from the text. This also includes finding and listing comparative statements (e.g., "Urban ecosystems have less biodiversity *compared to* rural habitats.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students exchange their graphic organizers with a partner. They compare their identified arguments, supporting evidence, and grammar examples, discussing any discrepancies. They practice asking "How has deforestation affected our area?" and "If we plant more trees, we can improve air quality" to discuss the content. Partners provide polite feedback, using phrases like, "I think this argument is clearer here..." or "Your example of the present perfect is very accurate."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary and Evaluation. In small groups (3-4 students), students synthesize the information from the article. They are tasked with creating a short, concise summary (5-7 sentences) of the article's main arguments about the ecological history of forests, focusing on the changes, impacts, and conservation efforts. They must also include a statement evaluating the effectiveness or impact of any mentioned conservation activities, using the target grammar (e.g., "Deforestation *has dramatically changed* the landscape, but conservation groups *have worked* to *plant* native *trees*.") and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> The groups will present their summary as if they are preparing a brief for a local environmental committee meeting about "Our Changing Forests." They should highlight key findings and suggest "If we continue these efforts, forest health *will improve*." or "If people *reduced* plastic waste, *marine life would recover*." They should also use community-focused language like, "Together, we can make a difference in conservation."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing the article's content, collaborative interaction to formulate arguments, and effective summarization. Students should aim to use appropriate pronunciation of words like "climate" and "biodiversity" and apply word stress correctly in compound terms like "plastic-free."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on group collaboration, clarity of argument summarization, and application of target language. A peer assessment checklist can also be used where groups evaluate each other's summaries based on specified criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their written summary (5-7 sentences) of the article's main arguments and their evaluation of environmental impacts/conservation efforts. They briefly present their summary orally to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in summarizing the article's main arguments (Reading comprehension).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., *deforestation, biodiversity, conservation, impact*) related to the theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of at least one Present Perfect, one First Conditional, and one Comparative structure from the curriculum.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative participation and polite expression of ideas within the group (Sociolinguistic).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation during oral presentation, especially of /aɪ/ and -ate endings.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one key argument about 'Our Changing Forests' that I learned from this article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy helped me the most today to understand complex information?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has my ability to summarize arguments from an article about environmental changes improved today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What other types of authentic non-fiction texts about forest health would I like to explore?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the reading comprehension activities worked particularly well for students at the B1.2 level? Were the articles challenging enough but accessible?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific reading strategies (e.g., scanning, identifying main ideas, analyzing arguments) need further reinforcement or scaffolding in future lessons? Did students successfully identify the grammatical structures?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson has provided students with a solid foundation of content and arguments about "Our Changing Forests" through reading. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the knowledge gained from this article to actively discuss, debate, and present their viewpoints on conservation efforts and environmental impact. They will use the target vocabulary and grammar to express their ideas orally, building on their comprehension of the text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>